#!/bin/sh

# THIS WARNS. IT DOES NOT BLOCK. Here is why, because the previous version
# blocked and the reasoning against it is specific rather than a preference.
#
# A push publishes COMMITS. These two scripts measure the WORKING TREE. Those
# are different things, and on 2026-09-23 they disagreed completely: a push of
# 115 committed, verified commits was refused because of an unused import in
# `src/programs/components/BuildYourWeek.tsx` — a file that was UNTRACKED, had
# never been committed, and was not in the push at all. It was another agent's
# half-written screen, open at that moment in the checkout all of us share.
#
# The version this replaces already knew: its own comment read "this is a fast
# local warning and CI is the gate". But blocking the push is exactly what stops
# CI — the thing it calls the gate — from ever running. That day it held up the
# first CI run this branch had ever had, across 115 commits, over one unused
# variable in a file nobody was pushing.
#
# Three agents share this working tree. Any one of them mid-save makes the tree
# an unreliable description of anyone's commits, and stashing to get a clean
# read is forbidden here for the same reason.
#
# So: still measured, still printed, never fatal.
#
# WHAT STILL BLOCKS, and should: `.husky/pre-commit` runs the ratchets at commit
# time, where the working tree genuinely is roughly what is being committed, and
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs the same two scripts against the PUSHED
# COMMIT, which is the exact measurement and the real gate.

echo "🔎 Ratchets (working tree — advisory; CI measures the pushed commit)"

failed=""
node scripts/typecheck-ratchet.mjs || failed="yes"
node scripts/lint-ratchet.mjs || failed="yes"

if [ -n "$failed" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

⚠️  A ratchet is unhappy with the WORKING TREE. Pushing anyway.

    This may be somebody else's uncommitted work, or your own — the tree does
    not distinguish, and in this checkout it is often not yours. Check whether
    the file named above is even in your commits:

        git status --porcelain <file>     # ?? means untracked, so not pushed
        git log --oneline -1 -- <file>    # empty means never committed

    CI runs the same two scripts against the pushed commit and WILL block on a
    real regression: .github/workflows/ci.yml

MSG
fi

exit 0
